Maven — the dominant build tool.

A real project is never a single java-compiler command. You have dozens of source files, third-party libraries — each with libraries of their own — tests to run, and a jar to package, all done identically on every developer's machine and on the build server. A build tool automates all of that, and for two decades, the default in the Java world has been Maven. This section is what Maven is, and the model behind it.

Maven is declarative. You don't script the build steps; you describe the project in one X-M-L file, called the P-O-M — the project object model — and Maven knows how to build it. The heart of that file is the list of dependencies. For each one, you give the group-I-D, the artifact-I-D, and the version — for example, Google's Guava library. You state what you depend on, and Maven works out how to get it and build with it.

That naming is worth dwelling on. Every library in the Java ecosystem is named by three coordinates — group-I-D, artifact-I-D, and version — which together form a globally unique address. Maven resolves those coordinates by downloading the library from a repository — by default, Maven Central — and caching it in a local folder, so it's fetched only once. And the decisive feature here is transitive dependencies: you ask for one library, and Maven automatically pulls in its dependencies, and theirs, resolving the whole tree for you. Declaring one line can bring in twenty jars, all correctly versioned.

Maven's other big idea is convention over configuration. Follow the standard project layout, and you write almost no build logic. Source goes in source-main-java, tests in source-test-java, resources in source-main-resources, and output in the target folder. Because every Maven project is arranged the same way, any Java developer can drop into any Maven project and know exactly where things live and how to build it. That shared convention is a real, and underrated, benefit.

You drive Maven by naming a lifecycle phase, and it runs that phase and every phase before it, in order: validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, deploy. So m-v-n package compiles your code, runs your tests, and — only if they pass — bundles the result into a jar in the target folder. M-v-n install does all that, and then copies the jar into your local repository, so your other projects can depend on it. The actual work in each phase is done by plugins — the compiler plugin, the Surefire test plugin, the jar plugin. Maven is really a framework for sequencing them.

The trade-off is Maven's whole character. The X-M-L is verbose, and doing anything outside the standard lifecycle can be awkward — Maven strongly prefers that you do things its way. In return, you get near-zero configuration for a conventional project, a vast plugin ecosystem, and a build that every Java developer already understands. When you want more programmability and speed than that rigidity allows, you reach for the script-based alternative — Gradle, which is next.
